</analysis>

<analysis>
Lastly, it's important to note that the application does not include any form of CSRF protection. Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) attacks can trick users into performing unintended actions on a web application during their authentic sessions, by exploiting vulnerabilities in applications that don't properly handle CSRF tokens. In this case, it is recommended to implement CSRF protection mechanisms like adding CSRF tokens to forms and verifying them upon submission. Confidence: Medium
</analysis>

