<analysis>
The given code snippet appears to be a Flask API endpoint for deleting a user by their ID. However, it contains a critical SQL injection vulnerability (CWE-89). 
The function directly concatenates the `user_id` parameter into the SQL query string without any sanitisation or parameterisation.
An attacker could manipulate the `user_id` parameter with malicious SQL code to delete arbitrary users or even execute other malicious queries. 
This can be exploited by sending a specially crafted `user_id` value that contains SQL code, such as "; DROP TABLE users; --" instead of an actual ID.

Additionally, I would like to point out that the function relies on the `jwt_required` decorator for authentication, but it does not perform any checks on the validity of the JWT token. 
This could potentially lead to a CWE-284 (Improper Access Control) vulnerability if an attacker were able to obtain or forge a valid JWT token.

Lastly, the code uses a direct SQL query without any prepared statements or parameterized queries, which is insecure and can lead to CWE-89 (SQL Injection).
</analysis>

