<analysis>
The given script is designed to continuously monitor the clipboard for new words and when it detects a change, sends GET requests to two online dictionary APIs (one for Merriam-Webster Dictionary API and one for Urban Dictionary API). The function uses `os.system` command to send notifications on the system which can lead to potential shell injection vulnerability if user input is directly concatenated without any sanitisation or parameterisation.

One more serious security risk lies in this script's usage of global variable 'word'. This could be exploited by an attacker to manipulate the data being checked, leading to incorrect results and/or unauthorised access. 

The script also doesn't seem to include any form of input validation or sanitisation on user-supplied inputs. The URLs it is sending requests to contain hardcoded values which could be exploited by an attacker to cause DoS, manipulate the server response and/or execute arbitrary commands on the target system.

The code doesn't seem to have any form of secure session management or encryption for its HTTPS requests. This means that sensitive information like cookies or authentication tokens may be exposed in plain text during these requests. 

Lastly, `os.system` usage can lead to remote code execution if user input is not correctly sanitised and controlled.
</analysis>

